/// ⭐ DB-353 §11.10 C1 — NO GRAFT-TIME DOOR READS THE BAND OR ITS POINTER (a census, run on every build of the tests).
///
/// Under the relay shape a node's band pointer is LOCAL progress: two honest nodes can stand at different pointers at the
/// same tip. A graft-time decision that consulted it would admit a block on one node and refuse it on another — exactly
/// what §11.10's "a node that cannot judge WAITS at its own fold, never excludes" rules out. So every band-dependent
/// verdict lives at the fold or in the pool. This census computes, over `node.rs`, the CLOSURE of functions that read the
/// band (directly — `band_door`, `band_root`, `band_root_at`, `band_pointer`, `band_drain`, `band_receive` — or by calling
/// one), never propagating through the fold pass (`fold_canonical_records`, where band reads belong), and asserts that no
/// graft-path entry point is in it.
/// UNIVERSE, stated: the named graft entry points below, and call edges spelled `name(` inside a body; a call through a
/// trait object or a fn pointer is not seen (none exists on these paths today). CALIBRATION: every named entry point is
/// FOUND (a rename cannot make the census vacuous), and the fold pass's own helper IS a reader (the scanner sees a real
/// band read where one exists).
/// TWO-PARTY-REVIEWED: a static census over one source file; the divergence it prevents is between two nodes, and the
/// two-node band pins (`db353_band_door_node_pins`) are its behavioural twin.
#[cfg(all(test, feature = "pins-node"))]
mod db353_c1_graft_reads_no_band_census;

